keel (0.15.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  The bugs of the maintainer's screenshots of the Core image, 2026-10-02.

  * inspect recognises a Keel image: /etc/turnkey_version on Keel Core says
    keel-core-19.0-trixie-amd64, which was "not a TurnKey version string",
    so the spec's header said "unknown appliance" (screenshots 047, 059).
    The keel- prefix is read like turnkey-.
  * instance.fqdn is no longer a required field of inspect. No first boot
    hook asks for it, apply leaves /etc/hosts alone without it, and an
    appliance out of the box has none, so inspect exited 13 on every new
    Core (048, 059) and confconsole's Database mode showed the error.
  * hub.api_key is the Keel Cloud key's file when the first boot stored
    one, {file: <secrets-dir>/cloud_api_key}, found by its presence alone;
    inspect said skip, looking only for a TurnKey Hub registration (058,
    061).
  * An allow-hotplug interface the live machine does not have is left out
    of the spec, with a report line: the eth1 stanza every image ships is
    a placeholder, and inspect and diff listed an eth1 the container does
    not have (034, 057). An auto interface whose card is missing is still
    reported; an offline root reports every stanza.
  * security.updates_at_first_boot is read from the line inithooks'
    95secupdates now leaves, /var/lib/inithooks/sec-updates (inithooks
    2.3.6+keel16), before the update posture: the posture is a cron-apt
    install action every image ships, so inspect said force after Skip
    (034, 058).
  * keel network confirm with nothing waiting says how the last change
    ended. Each change's outcome, confirmed or reverted, is kept in
    /var/lib/keel/network/last.json (0600) with its file and time; a
    change another session confirmed is "already confirmed ... it stays"
    and exits 0, one that reverted is called reverted (exit 21). It always
    said "one not confirmed in time has been reverted", false after a
    confirmation from another session (040). The outcome is recorded
    only once the marker is gone and the timers are disarmed, and a
    record that cannot be written (a full disk) is said and never fails
    the confirmation or the revert, so it can never leave a confirmed
    change to revert. Arming a new change removes the previous record,
    so an older confirmation never answers for a change whose end
    recorded nothing (a revert whose saved copy was missing); a record
    that cannot be removed refuses the change before anything moves.
  * monit's interface is set in keel.conf: set httpd unixsocket
    /run/monit.sock uid root gid root permission 0600, allow localhost. A
    Unix socket only root opens, no network listener (decision 0021).
    Without it monit summary and monit status always failed (069, 073,
    096).
  * inspect reads the monitor's channels back from /etc/keel/monitor.json
    (keel#60): email, telegram, ntfy, webhook and details, token and URL
    files by reference, never by value, and the report line names only
    the channels. The emitted spec of a monitored machine validates and
    re-applies with no change. diff now withholds observed channel values
    too, declared or not, as it did the declared ones.
  * A CrowdSec online_api_credentials.yaml of comments only counts as
    absent, not as a registration (keel#61, item 1): the Core build seeds
    such a file, and one left in an image kept cscli capi register from
    ever running. An empty file stays "not registered yet".

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 06:00:00 +0000

keel (0.15.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The overlay manifest gains hooks.state: {path, timeout} (keel#62, the
    handbook's erratum of 0041): path is the overlay's
    /usr/lib/keel/overlays/<name>/state, which manifest validate checks
    exists, is executable, root's and not writable by group or others;
    timeout is 1 to 600 seconds, 120 by default. An appliance has none.
  * apply --system runs an overlay's state hooks (keel#62, item 1; step 7
    of decision 0041): the declared hooks.state, then every executable of
    /usr/lib/keel/overlays/<name>/state.d/ in name order, each with
    enabled or disabled and within its timeout (killed with its session
    and failing the step on expiry), after the overlay's units come up
    and before they go down. An overlay with no unit but a hook, Coraza's
    module, now gets a step: before, turning it on wrote Monit's
    waf-blocks check and loaded nothing. A hook or its directory that is
    not root's, is writable by group or others, or is a link out of
    /usr/lib/keel/overlays refuses the step; an undeclared state file is
    not run. keel records the state and a digest of the hooks (paths and
    content hashes) in /var/lib/keel/overlays/<name> once every hook
    passed and runs them again when either differs, so a second apply
    changes nothing and a hook installed later still runs; a failed hook
    fails the step and records nothing. Under --root no hook runs, and
    the step says only that.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000

keel (0.14.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The spec gains firewall.enabled, allowed in a cloud advanced
    installation only (decision 0041, as the maintainer scoped it on
    2026-09-30): apply --system then derives an nftables ruleset from the
    manifests' exposure classes (public ports everywhere, mesh ports on
    the WireGuard interface only), writes it to
    /etc/keel/firewall/keel-manifest.nft and loads it as the table inet
    keel, which the new keel-firewall.service loads again at boot. It
    always lets in the loopback, established traffic, ICMPv6, the DHCP
    client replies, and DNS and DHCP from the guests of the host's NAT
    bridges (veth and tap ports only, no default route; never an uplink
    bridge such as a Proxmox vmbr0). It is refused unless it opens every
    port sshd listens on, read from sshd -T and an active ssh.socket and
    never guessed; it is left alone while a network change waits for its
    confirmation; and it is checked with nft -c as a copy that only then
    replaces the file. enabled: false removes keel's file and table.
    inspect writes firewall.enabled back; diff compares the file and, on
    the live system, the table the kernel holds. Suggests nftables.

