keel (0.13.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The instance spec gains appliance, installation and overlays (decision
    0041, step 3 of its first implementation). A spec that names an
    appliance is held against the manifests installed under the root:
    every overlay of the resolved chain is written out, enabled or
    disabled, and the requires of an enabled overlay are enabled (rule
    25); a secret a manifest declares is a known name, rendered as
    KEEL_SECRET_<NAME>, and generate is refused where the manifest says
    never (rule 26); app.options are the declared options, of their
    type, with every required one present (rule 27). on, off and ask are
    refused with the reason. spec validate takes --root.
  * apply --system converges each overlay's units: enabled enables and
    starts them, disabled stops and disables them, in requires order.
    Nothing is masked or unmasked; a masked unit is refused. CrowdSec's
    identity is made on its first enable (tracker#47): cscli machines
    add, an empty online_api_credentials.yaml, cscli capi register, whose
    failure offline is said and survived since the local API runs
    without it, and cscli bouncers add, whose key goes from its standard
    output to the bouncer's .local file, mode 0600, and nowhere else. No
    identity is made under --root. The bouncer always runs in nftables
    mode: its iptables mode, which the Debian postinst picks under
    TurnKey's iptables-legacy, needs ipset, which nothing installs.
  * apply --system writes Monit's checks derived from the manifests to
    /etc/keel/monit/keel-manifest.conf: the appliance's own processes and
    checks and those of every enabled overlay, restarted through systemd
    within each process's restart limit, and told through keel notify,
    whose new checks service and restarts name the unit and where to
    look. The file is always written and included from /etc/monit/conf.d
    only while monitor.enabled is true, then checked with monit -t and
    monit reloaded.
  * inspect writes appliance.name, installation.mode (from the spec the
    installer emitted) and the overlays' states from systemd. diff
    compares the states with systemd and the derived Monit file and its
    include with what apply renders, so a hand edit of either is drift.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000

keel (0.12.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New module keel.manifest and commands keel manifest validate and keel
    manifest show: the reader of the appliance manifest, version 1
    (decision 0041, step 1 of its first implementation). One schema for
    overlay manifests, /usr/share/keel/overlays/<name>.yaml, and
    appliance manifests, /usr/share/keel/appliances/<name>.yaml, read
    under --root; unknown keys are refused at every level and every
    error is reported at once, with the exit codes of spec validate.
  * validate checks rules 1 to 24 of the format, and resolves each
    appliance along its base chain down to core: overlays once, every
    (port, protocol) once whatever the states, requires enabled in every
    mode, names once across the chain. A state written on, off, yes or
    no reaches PyYAML as a boolean and is refused with that reason; a
    key given twice in one mapping is refused rather than overwritten.
    A unit name has the shape systemd.unit(5) gives it, so none climbs
    out of the unit directories with "..", and an init script counts
    only as a regular executable file inside the root. Names and values
    are matched whole, so a trailing newline is refused. YAML nested past
    the recursion limit cannot be read (exit 2).
  * show prints a manifest, and with --resolved the overlays with their
    state per installation mode, the ports with their loopback, mesh or
    public exposure, the processes, checks, secrets and hooks of the
    chain. On Keel Web its first two tables are the format's.
  * Nothing else in keel reads a manifest yet.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000

keel (0.11.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * apply makes the MariaDB server it converges answer before asking it
    anything: one that is starting is waited for, one that is stopped is
    started with systemctl start, both within one bound of 300 s. At
    first boot inithooks.service is not ordered after mariadb.service,
    so the hook 10keel-system asked the server while it was starting,
    the field was refused, the hook carried on as a first boot hook
    must, and the appliance kept server_id = 1 until apply ran again.
  * A server that does not answer within the bound, whose start fails,
    or that is active and refuses the client fails the field with that
    reason: nothing is written or restarted and the run exits non zero,
    so the next apply takes the field up again. A dry run starts no
    server, and under --root none is asked, started or waited for.
  * Before the wait, apply prints one line on the terminal (standard
    output when it is one, else /dev/tty, where the first boot dialogs
    draw), never into the output 10keel-system reads through a pipe, so
    tty1 no longer stays blank while the server starts.
  * A declared server whose unit is disabled is enabled, a line of the
    plan, so it starts at boot and not only when apply starts it.

