keel (0.11.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A MariaDB replica is read only (tracker#26). The replica's drop-in
    says read_only = ON, so the application on a replica can no longer
    write rows of its own: one WordPress page request inserted a
    wp_navigation post, the primary's next write reused its id, and the
    SQL thread stopped with error 1062. The replication thread and root
    write through it (READ_ONLY ADMIN, measured on MariaDB 11.8, where
    SUPER alone no longer does); the application's account holds
    privileges on its own schema only and is refused with error 1290.
    The restart that turns it on comes before the seed, whose load runs
    as root.
  * On a replica, after the seed, apply revokes READ_ONLY ADMIN with
    sql_log_bin off from every account that holds it but root, mysql
    and mariadb.sys at this machine: the image's admin and Adminer's
    adminer hold ALL ON *.*, and Adminer could write to the replica
    through them. The accounts are recorded in
    /var/lib/keel/database/read-only-admin first, and a promotion or a
    return to standalone or primary grants it back to those only. root,
    and Webmin's MySQL module with it, still writes: MariaDB 11.8 has no
    way to stop it, and docs/apply.md says so.
  * apply sets read_only with SET GLOBAL when the file already says so
    and the server does not (turned off by hand, or a standalone that
    was a replica), and says so when the server names no read_only.
  * keel database promote no longer loses what the replica received and
    had not applied: it stops the I/O thread, waits up to 600 s for the
    SQL thread to apply the relay log, and only then runs STOP SLAVE,
    RESET SLAVE ALL and turns read_only off. A SQL thread stopped on an
    error is refused with what to do, and one that stops or does not
    catch up while draining starts the I/O thread again, says whether
    that worked, and promotes nothing. The read_only line leaves the
    drop-in without a restart, before READ_ONLY ADMIN is granted back,
    so a failed GRANT cannot leave the new primary read only at its
    next restart.
  * keel inspect reports database.server.read_only, with the accounts
    that write through it, and keel diff compares it with the role the
    server has (decision 0020), not with a field of the description.
  * docs/apply.md: a replica is a warm standby and must not receive
    traffic until promoted, and what WordPress shows on one.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel (0.11.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Seeding a replica reads the primary's accounts before the dump, and
    again after it, and refuses when the two readings differ. Read only
    after the dump, a CREATE USER in between was applied twice and
    stopped the replica; read only before, it was never applied.
  * An account the replica holds with other grants than the primary's
    has its grants made the primary's, with sql_log_bin off, before
    replication starts: the primary's next REVOKE of a grant the replica
    lacked stopped it ("There is no such grant"). It keeps the replica's
    own authentication, since every node makes its own application
    password at first boot, and the plan names such accounts: a later
    ALTER USER of them on the primary replaces the replica's password,
    and the replica's application configuration must follow.
  * A primary that holds roles, or grants privileges to PUBLIC, is
    refused while apply observes the replica, before anything changes.
    Roles are not copied: a user whose privileges come through a role
    was copied with none, silently, and a change to a role stopped the
    replica.
  * The server id of a node with a WireGuard overlay is derived from its
    machine-id, its overlay address(es) and the listen entries that name
    it, leaving out wildcards and loopback: listen is :: on every node,
    so two nodes built from one image took the same server id. A node
    without an overlay keeps the server id it had. docs/apply.md says
    what changes a server id and what that does to a running pair.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 10:40:00 +0000

keel (0.11.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A new MariaDB replica is seeded with a copy of its primary before it
    replicates. It used to start from an empty gtid_slave_pos, so only
    what the primary wrote afterwards arrived: a WordPress replica had
    no wp_* tables, and the first UPDATE of an older post stopped its
    SQL thread. apply --system now pulls mariadb-dump
    --single-transaction --gtid --master-data=2 from the primary, as the
    replication account, drops the local databases the operator
    confirmed only once the copy is on disk, loads it and starts
    replication at the dump's GTID position (MASTER_USE_GTID=slave_pos).
    The password reaches the clients in an options file of mode 0600,
    created exclusively and removed afterwards, never an argument.
  * The primary is asked first, while apply observes the machine: an
    unreachable primary, or one that does not grant the copy, is refused
    before the replica's configuration is rewritten or anything dropped.
    A replica already replicating healthily from the declared primary is
    never dialled or copied again.
  * A primary grants the replication account SELECT, SHOW VIEW, TRIGGER
    and EVENT beside REPLICATION SLAVE, which is what the dump needs and
    no more than the binary log already carries to it. GRANT only adds,
    so a primary set up by an older keel gains them at its next apply.
  * The primary's accounts come with the copy: the ones the replica
    lacks, other than root, mysql, mariadb.sys, debian-sys-maint, repl,
    the anonymous account and roles, are read with SHOW CREATE USER and
    SHOW GRANTS and created with sql_log_bin off before replication
    starts, so an ALTER USER of the application's database user no
    longer stops the replica.
  * The seed is refused before anything is dropped when the data
    directory has less free space than twice the dump.
  * docs/apply.md states what the grant exposes: repl reads every table
    of the primary, password hashes included, and why that is the grant
    rather than one per schema.
  * A replica whose SQL thread stopped is refused with the server's
    error, and rebuilt from a fresh copy with --destroy-local-database:
    the way out for a replica 0.11.2 or older built empty.

